    }
  ],
  "cues": [
    {
      "start": 0,
      "end": 0.8199999928474426,
      "text": "Welcome back.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 1.340000033378601,
      "end": 2.5999999046325684,
      "text": "We have learned to draw forces.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 3.140000104904175,
      "end": 7.360000133514404,
      "text": "Now let us use those diagrams to solve richer problems, without collecting a",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 7.360000133514404,
      "end": 8.720000267028809,
      "text": "mountain of unrelated formulas.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 9.680000305175781,
      "end": 10.539999961853027,
      "text": "First, momentum.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 11.4399995803833,
      "end": 14.279999732971191,
      "text": "For a constant mass, momentum is mass times velocity.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 14.979999542236328,
      "end": 17.040000915527344,
      "text": "It is a vector, so direction matters.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 18.059999465942383,
      "end": 19.799999237060547,
      "text": "Impulse tells us how much momentum changes.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 20.520000457763672,
      "end": 23.540000915527344,
      "text": "For a constant force, impulse is force times the time interval.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 24.260000228881836,
      "end": 28.34000015258789,
      "text": "For a changing force, it is the signed area under the force versus time graph.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 29.8799991607666,
      "end": 32.939998626708984,
      "text": "Imagine a two kilogram ball moving right at three meters per second.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 33.560001373291016,
      "end": 36.31999969482422,
      "text": "After a hit, it moves left at two meters per second.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 37.08000183105469,
      "end": 38.119998931884766,
      "text": "Choose right as positive.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 38.7599983215332,
      "end": 40,
      "text": "What is its change in momentum?",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 40.720001220703125,
      "end": 41.2599983215332,
      "text": "Take a moment.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 42.18000030517578,
      "end": 43.52000045776367,
      "text": "Initial momentum is plus six.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 44.18000030517578,
      "end": 45.599998474121094,
      "text": "Final momentum is minus four.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 46.279998779296875,
      "end": 50.400001525878906,
      "text": "Final minus initial gives minus ten kilogram meters per second, or minus ten",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 50.400001525878906,
      "end": 51,
      "text": "newton seconds.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 51.34000015258789,
      "end": 56.2599983215332,
      "text": "If the hit lasts zero point two seconds, the average net force is minus fifty",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 56.2599983215332,
      "end": 56.599998474121094,
      "text": "newtons.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 57.63999938964844,
      "end": 59.08000183105469,
      "text": "Subtracting speeds would miss the reversal.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 59.959999084472656,
      "end": 61.720001220703125,
      "text": "Now look at a triangular net force pulse.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 62.5,
      "end": 65.95999908447266,
      "text": "Its base is zero point four seconds and its peak is twenty newtons.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 66.77999877929688,
      "end": 70.31999969482422,
      "text": "The area is one half times base times height, or four newton seconds.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 71.12000274658203,
      "end": 74.6044455634223,
      "text": "For a half kilogram body, this gives an eight meter per second change in",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 74.6044455634223,
      "end": 75.04000091552734,
      "text": "velocity.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 75.81999969482422,
      "end": 77.16000366210938,
      "text": "Finally, choose a whole system.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 78.13999938964844,
      "end": 80.87999725341797,
      "text": "Internal interaction forces cancel in its total momentum equation.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 81.4800033569336,
      "end": 85.73999786376953,
      "text": "When the net external impulse is zero, total momentum stays constant, although",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 85.73999786376953,
      "end": 87.33999633789062,
      "text": "each object's momentum can change.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 88.0199966430664,
      "end": 91.77999877929688,
      "text": "A short collision may let us neglect a small external impulse, but we must",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 91.77999877929688,
      "end": 92.72000122070312,
      "text": "state that approximation.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 93.62000274658203,
      "end": 98.22000122070312,
      "text": "In JEE, first decide which body or system your momentum equation describes.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 98.85600000000285,
      "end": 102.23600011444377,
      "text": "Let us pause the motion and study a knot in equilibrium.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 103.15600019073771,
      "end": 106.01599984741496,
      "text": "A light knot carries a downward load of one hundred newtons.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 106.9359999237089,
      "end": 108.61600022888469,
      "text": "One rope pulls it horizontally left.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 109.61600022888469,
      "end": 112.45600038147258,
      "text": "Another pulls up and right at thirty degrees above the horizontal.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 113.25599961853312,
      "end": 117.33599954223918,
      "text": "The ropes are taut, and we treat the knot as a particle with negligible mass.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 117.91599946594523,
      "end": 119.51599984741496,
      "text": "Which rope supports the vertical load?",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 120.53600030517863,
      "end": 121.8960009155302,
      "text": "Picture the arrows before answering.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 122.5560007629423,
      "end": 124.57599931335734,
      "text": "Only the inclined rope has an upward component.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 125.37600045776652,
      "end": 126.31599908447551,
      "text": "Call its tension T.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 126.7760000762968,
      "end": 131.25779396898602,
      "text": "Its upward component is T sine thirty degrees, and its rightward component is",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 131.25779396898602,
      "end": 131.3159990844755,
      "text": "T",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 131.3159990844755,
      "end": 132.33599954223916,
      "text": "cosine thirty degrees.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 133.23600106811807,
      "end": 137.0560007629423,
      "text": "Vertical equilibrium says T sine thirty equals one hundred.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 137.0560007629423,
      "end": 140.0560007629423,
      "text": "Sine thirty is one half, so T is two hundred newtons.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 140.83599954223916,
      "end": 143.2160006103544,
      "text": "The horizontal rope must balance the rightward component.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 143.8159990844755,
      "end": 147.60061658184628,
      "text": "Its tension is two hundred times cosine thirty, or one hundred times the",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 147.60061658184628,
      "end": 147.91600137329385,
      "text": "square",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 147.91600137329385,
      "end": 151.15599923706338,
      "text": "root of three newtons, about one hundred seventy three point two.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 151.8159990844755,
      "end": 152.79599862671182,
      "text": "Notice something interesting.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 153.25600152588174,
      "end": 155.07600122070596,
      "text": "The inclined tension exceeds the weight.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 155.4759989318876,
      "end": 158.75600152588174,
      "text": "That is not a mistake: only part of this tension acts upward.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 159.8159990844755,
      "end": 161.03600030517862,
      "text": "Try changing the angle in your mind.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 161.59600167846963,
      "end": 165.25600152588174,
      "text": "A more nearly horizontal supporting rope needs a larger tension to provide the",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 165.25600152588174,
      "end": 166.2960024414091,
      "text": "same vertical component.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 167.2960024414091,
      "end": 171.43600183105752,
      "text": "For concurrent forces on a particle, setting both component sums to zero gives",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 171.43600183105752,
      "end": 171.93600183105752,
      "text": "equilibrium.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 172.7160006103544,
      "end": 176.95599847412393,
      "text": "For an extended rigid body, zero net force alone is not enough; torque must",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 176.95599847412393,
      "end": 177.67599969482706,
      "text": "balance too.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 178.1359987792997,
      "end": 179.27599816894815,
      "text": "We study that in rotation.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 179.93600183105752,
      "end": 183.53600030517862,
      "text": "In an exam, mark whether an angle is measured from the horizontal or vertical.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 184.4159975585966,
      "end": 187.75600152588174,
      "text": "Swapping sine and cosine without checking the drawing is a very avoidable lost",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 187.75600152588174,
      "end": 188.03600030517862,
      "text": "mark.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 188.75600152588174,
      "end": 191.87599664306924,
      "text": "Your diagram is doing the thinking before your calculator does the arithmetic.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 192.4560000000055,
      "end": 196.7577719606865,
      "text": "Here is an Atwood machine: two hanging masses connected by a rope over a",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 196.7577719606865,
      "end": 197.17599979019715,
      "text": "pulley.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 197.63599982834413,
      "end": 201.8959995803888,
      "text": "Let the masses be three kilograms and two kilograms, and use g equal to ten",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 201.8959995803888,
      "end": 202.91600003815248,
      "text": "meters per second squared.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 203.8959995803888,
      "end": 206.3959995803888,
      "text": "The rope is light, taut and inextensible.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 207.19599977112367,
      "end": 209.03599992371156,
      "text": "The pulley is massless and frictionless.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 209.67599931336,
      "end": 212.236000686651,
      "text": "These assumptions are part of the problem, not decorations.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 213.09599938965394,
      "end": 215.99600091553285,
      "text": "The rope constraint makes the acceleration magnitudes equal.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 216.7960001525934,
      "end": 219.13600030518128,
      "text": "One mass moves downward while the other moves upward.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 219.75599923706605,
      "end": 223.65600076294496,
      "text": "Our ideal pulley and rope also make the tension equal on both sides.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 224.7960001525934,
      "end": 226.43599954224183,
      "text": "Draw two separate free body diagrams.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 227.37599816895082,
      "end": 232.07599893189027,
      "text": "On the heavier mass, choose downward as positive: thirty minus T equals three",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 232.07599893189027,
      "end": 232.59599938965394,
      "text": "a.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 232.59599938965394,
      "end": 237.9560000000055,
      "text": "On the lighter mass, choose upward as positive: T minus twenty equals two a.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 237.9560000000055,
      "end": 240.97600045776917,
      "text": "We have chosen different positive directions because the bodies move",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 240.97600045776917,
      "end": 241.47600045776917,
      "text": "oppositely.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 242.43599954224183,
      "end": 243.09599938965394,
      "text": "Add the equations.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 243.7960001525934,
      "end": 247.0360018310602,
      "text": "Tension disappears, leaving ten equals five a.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 247.0360018310602,
      "end": 249.25599923706605,
      "text": "The acceleration is two meters per second squared.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 250.41599908447816,
      "end": 251.27599969482972,
      "text": "Now predict the tension.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 251.93599954224183,
      "end": 253.7159983215387,
      "text": "Is it automatically the weight of either mass?",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 254.6960016784723,
      "end": 255.33600106812074,
      "text": "Think for a moment.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 255.93599954224183,
      "end": 258.03600183106016,
      "text": "No, because both masses accelerate.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 259.03600183106016,
      "end": 262.61600366211485,
      "text": "Substitute into the lighter mass equation: T minus twenty equals four.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 263.3759981689508,
      "end": 264.6760012207086,
      "text": "T is twenty four newtons.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 265.65599694824766,
      "end": 269.8113838485381,
      "text": "Check the heavier mass too: thirty minus twenty four equals six, which is",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 269.8113838485381,
      "end": 270.0959993896539,
      "text": "three",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 270.0959993896539,
      "end": 270.65599694824766,
      "text": "times two.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 271.41599908447813,
      "end": 272.5959993896539,
      "text": "Both diagrams agree.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 272.5959993896539,
      "end": 277.5159975585992,
      "text": "Adding equations is useful for finding acceleration; returning to one body",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 277.5159975585992,
      "end": 278.13600030518126,
      "text": "gives tension.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 279.0159975585992,
      "end": 282.71600213623594,
      "text": "If the pulley has rotational inertia, its two tensions need not be equal.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 283.61600366211485,
      "end": 287.3759981689508,
      "text": "If a rope goes slack, the common acceleration constraint stops applying.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 288.55599847412657,
      "end": 292.65599694824766,
      "text": "JEE connected body problems become manageable when you separate actual forces",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 292.65599694824766,
      "end": 294.8260027465875,
      "text": "from the geometric constraint connecting the bodies.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 295.0560000000085,
      "end": 298.1559999046411,
      "text": "Friction deserves a decision, not just a substitution.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 298.91599989510433,
      "end": 301.99600005722897,
      "text": "Take a four kilogram block on a level floor, initially at rest.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 302.7159998474206,
      "end": 306.8359997329797,
      "text": "Use g equal to ten, a static coefficient of zero point five, and a kinetic",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 306.8359997329797,
      "end": 308.41599965668576,
      "text": "coefficient of zero point three.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 308.97600007630245,
      "end": 312.7159998474206,
      "text": "With no other vertical forces, the normal reaction is forty newtons.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 312.7159998474206,
      "end": 316.0760004577722,
      "text": "The maximum static friction is twenty newtons.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 316.0760004577722,
      "end": 317.9559996185388,
      "text": "Apply an eighteen newton horizontal push.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 318.6159994659509,
      "end": 320.15600038147824,
      "text": "Does friction become twenty?",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 320.15600038147824,
      "end": 321.01599908448117,
      "text": "Have a little think.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 321.01599908448117,
      "end": 321.69599938965695,
      "text": "No.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 322.2360003051843,
      "end": 326.3960001525964,
      "text": "It adjusts to eighteen newtons, opposing the tendency to slide, and the block",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 326.3960001525964,
      "end": 327.1759989318933,
      "text": "remains at rest.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 327.9759981689538,
      "end": 329.8960001525964,
      "text": "Now apply twenty four newtons.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 329.8960001525964,
      "end": 331.5560000000085,
      "text": "Static friction cannot maintain rest.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 332.19599938965695,
      "end": 336.27600122071163,
      "text": "Once sliding begins, the kinetic friction magnitude is twelve newtons.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 336.27600122071163,
      "end": 340.53599954224484,
      "text": "The net horizontal force is twelve, so acceleration is three meters per second",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 340.53599954224484,
      "end": 340.85599923706906,
      "text": "squared.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 341.4959986267175,
      "end": 345.3359987793054,
      "text": "The coefficient tells us a limit or a sliding model; it does not replace",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 345.3359987793054,
      "end": 346.9959986267175,
      "text": "identifying the situation.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 346.9959986267175,
      "end": 348.6360018310632,
      "text": "What if the pull is angled upward?",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 349.35599923706906,
      "end": 352.5513849010064,
      "text": "Its vertical component reduces the normal reaction while contact is",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 352.5513849010064,
      "end": 353.0760004577722,
      "text": "maintained.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 353.9160006103601,
      "end": 356.2360003051843,
      "text": "Recalculate the normal force before calculating friction.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 357.01599908448117,
      "end": 360.99600244141476,
      "text": "On a stationary block on a simple incline, the required friction is m g sine",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 360.99600244141476,
      "end": 364.09600091553585,
      "text": "theta and the normal reaction is m g cosine theta.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 364.79599786377804,
      "end": 368.4359972534265,
      "text": "Rest is possible when tangent theta is no greater than the static coefficient.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 369.09600091553585,
      "end": 370.816002136239,
      "text": "Now distinguish sliding from rolling.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 371.4359972534265,
      "end": 374.9718968803295,
      "text": "In ideal pure rolling, the contact point does not slip relative to the",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 374.9718968803295,
      "end": 375.37599969483273,
      "text": "surface.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 375.37599969483273,
      "end": 379.4759981689538,
      "text": "Static friction may act, but it is not automatically at its limiting value.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 380.09600091553585,
      "end": 383.9160006103601,
      "text": "A freely rolling wheel on an ideal horizontal surface can have zero friction.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 384.7160036621179,
      "end": 388.51599908448117,
      "text": "Real rolling resistance comes from effects such as deformation and is not",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 388.51599908448117,
      "end": 390.01599908448117,
      "text": "generally the kinetic friction formula.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 390.7160036621179,
      "end": 395.6006134714065,
      "text": "For JEE, name the contact, find its slipping tendency, and only then choose",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 395.6006134714065,
      "end": 395.79599786377804,
      "text": "the",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 395.79599786377804,
      "end": 396.1360018310632,
      "text": "friction equation.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 396.40800000001144,
      "end": 399.58800006676864,
      "text": "A car turns left on a level road at constant speed.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 400.22799993325424,
      "end": 401.467999942791,
      "text": "Is its acceleration zero?",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 402.3280000763054,
      "end": 405.7080001907463,
      "text": "Its speed stays constant, but its velocity direction changes.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 406.3280000763054,
      "end": 409.6079998092766,
      "text": "It needs an inward acceleration of v squared divided by r.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 410.3680000381584,
      "end": 412.8679990844841,
      "text": "Now ask which real force supplies that acceleration.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 413.52800083924484,
      "end": 417.9280004577751,
      "text": "In a simplified level road model, static friction between the tires and road",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 417.9280004577751,
      "end": 419.8079996185417,
      "text": "supplies the horizontal inward force.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 420.56799984742355,
      "end": 422.4879999237175,
      "text": "Weight and the normal reaction balance vertically.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 423.1080007629509,
      "end": 425.90800000001144,
      "text": "There is no separate force to add called centripetal force.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 425.90800000001144,
      "end": 429.32799816895675,
      "text": "That phrase describes the job done by the inward net force.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 429.8679990844841,
      "end": 432.8479986267204,
      "text": "With no other vertical forces, N equals m g.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 433.4480009155388,
      "end": 436.4480009155388,
      "text": "The required friction is m v squared over r.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 437.00799847413253,
      "end": 439.88799954224777,
      "text": "Its available maximum is mu s times m g.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 440.48800183106613,
      "end": 446.46800137330246,
      "text": "The no skid condition is therefore m v squared over r no greater than mu s m",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 446.66799832154464,
      "end": 446.98800183106613,
      "text": "g.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 447.62800122071457,
      "end": 452.8679990844841,
      "text": "Mass cancels, giving a maximum speed equal to the square root of mu s r g.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 453.3479986267204,
      "end": 458.207999237072,
      "text": "Try our example: coefficient zero point five, turn radius twenty meters, and g",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 458.207999237072,
      "end": 458.9280004577751,
      "text": "equal to ten.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 459.62800122071457,
      "end": 461.5280027465935,
      "text": "Under the square root we have one hundred.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 461.5280027465935,
      "end": 463.7879972534294,
      "text": "The maximum speed is ten meters per second.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 464.3480024414177,
      "end": 467.5280027465935,
      "text": "At six meters per second, is friction already at its maximum?",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 468.4679975586052,
      "end": 468.50799847413253,
      "text": "Pause.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 469.00799847413253,
      "end": 469.30800152589035,
      "text": "No.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 469.82799816895675,
      "end": 473.6680021362419,
      "text": "For a one thousand kilogram car it is one thousand times thirty six divided by",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 473.6680021362419,
      "end": 477.38800335694503,
      "text": "twenty, or eighteen hundred newtons, below the five thousand newton limit.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 477.62800122071457,
      "end": 482.1680021362419,
      "text": "For this calculation we neglect air forces, road irregularities and vehicle",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 482.1680021362419,
      "end": 482.5680036621208,
      "text": "details.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 483.1680021362419,
      "end": 485.2879972534294,
      "text": "It is a physics model, not driving advice.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 486.00799847413253,
      "end": 489.00799847413253,
      "text": "Smaller radius means a tighter turn and a lower limiting speed.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 489.60799694825363,
      "end": 493.55748527019375,
      "text": "When you see a circular motion question, draw the actual forces first and",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 493.55748527019375,
      "end": 493.82799816895675,
      "text": "write",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 493.82799816895675,
      "end": 497.4480009155388,
      "text": "their inward components equal to m v squared over r.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 497.56800000001437,
      "end": 500.84799997140414,
      "text": "A banked road tilts upward toward the outside of a turn.",
      "chapter": 5
    },
    {
      "start": 501.5879999809409,
      "end": 505.6680003814841,
      "text": "This tilts the normal reaction inward, so part of that force can turn the car",
      "chapter": 5
    },
    {
      "start": 505.6680003814841,
      "end": 506.7880002670432,
      "text": "even without friction.",
      "chapter": 5
    },
    {
      "start": 507.9480001144553,
      "end": 512.5280000381613,
      "text": "Draw a cross section: inward is horizontal toward the center, while weight",
      "chapter": 5
    },
    {
      "start": 512.5280000381613,
      "end": 513.8280002288961,
      "text": "remains vertically downward.",
      "chapter": 5
    },
    {
      "start": 514.4080001526022,
      "end": 516.588000457778,
      "text": "Let theta be the road's angle above horizontal.",
      "chapter": 5
    },
    {
      "start": 517.2680007629538,
      "end": 520.168000381484,
      "text": "The normal reaction makes the same angle with the vertical.",
      "chapter": 5
    },
    {
      "start": 520.7079993896627,
      "end": 525.168000381484,
      "text": "In the ideal frictionless model, its vertical component is N cosine theta.",
      "chapter": 5
    },
    {
      "start": 525.168000381484,
      "end": 529.4880000763083,
      "text": "That balances m g because the car has no vertical acceleration.",
      "chapter": 5
    },
    {
      "start": 530.527999084487,
      "end": 532.7279998474264,
      "text": "Its inward component is N sine theta.",
      "chapter": 5
    },
    {
      "start": 533.3080016784811,
      "end": 534.9080001526022,
      "text": "That equals m v squared over r.",
      "chapter": 5
    },
    {
      "start": 535.588000457778,
      "end": 537.7480003051901,
      "text": "Divide the inward equation by the vertical equation.",
      "chapter": 5
    },
    {
      "start": 538.8080016784811,
      "end": 540.1879989318991,
      "text": "Both N and mass disappear.",
      "chapter": 5
    },
    {
      "start": 541.027999084487,
      "end": 543.9280006103659,
      "text": "Tangent theta equals v squared divided by r g.",
      "chapter": 5
    },
    {
      "start": 543.9280006103659,
      "end": 547.148001831069,
      "text": "So the ideal speed is the square root of r g tangent theta.",
      "chapter": 5
    },
    {
      "start": 548.088000457778,
      "end": 551.9480010681295,
      "text": "Take a twenty meter radius, a forty five degree bank and g equal to ten.",
      "chapter": 5
    },
    {
      "start": 552.5079986267233,
      "end": 556.7079993896627,
      "text": "Tangent forty five equals one, giving ten times the square root of two meters",
      "chapter": 5
    },
    {
      "start": 556.7079993896627,
      "end": 559.9080001526022,
      "text": "per second, about fourteen point one four.",
      "chapter": 5
    },
    {
      "start": 559.9080001526022,
      "end": 563.8079978637838,
      "text": "This is the speed requiring no friction in our model, not a universal maximum.",
      "chapter": 5
    },
    {
      "start": 564.5480033569479,
      "end": 565.8479987793112,
      "text": "What happens below that speed?",
      "chapter": 5
    },
    {
      "start": 566.5480033569479,
      "end": 568.7079993896627,
      "text": "Think about a nearly stationary car on the slope.",
      "chapter": 5
    },
    {
      "start": 569.2679969482565,
      "end": 572.8280021362448,
      "text": "It tends to slide down the bank, so static friction acts up the bank.",
      "chapter": 5
    },
    {
      "start": 573.8079978637838,
      "end": 577.5879966430807,
      "text": "Above the ideal speed, the tendency reverses and friction acts down the bank.",
      "chapter": 5
    },
    {
      "start": 578.2079993896627,
      "end": 581.6880027465963,
      "text": "Resolve its vertical and inward components as well as the normal reaction.",
      "chapter": 5
    },
    {
      "start": 582.5879966430807,
      "end": 586.9879981689596,
      "text": "Do not assume N equals m g on a bank or insert mu N before checking whether",
      "chapter": 5
    },
    {
      "start": 586.9879981689596,
      "end": 587.8479987793112,
      "text": "friction is limiting.",
      "chapter": 5
    },
    {
      "start": 588.8879996948385,
      "end": 591.7679969482565,
      "text": "Wider speed range problems need those full component equations.",
      "chapter": 5
    },
    {
      "start": 592.7280036621237,
      "end": 596.7679969482565,
      "text": "Our toolkit now connects impulse, equilibrium, constraints and curved motion.",
      "chapter": 5
    },
    {
      "start": 597.7679969482565,
      "end": 601.3887993896647,
      "text": "Try the original practice questions next; they are JEE style, not claimed past",
      "chapter": 5
    },
    {
      "start": 601.3887993896647,
      "end": 601.5360000000175,
      "text": "paper questions.",
      "chapter": 5
    }
  ]
}
